// ONE written @rit and ONE written @!rit, in a section the form plays TWICE. Both
// playings must draw the same spanner, because it is one written pair.
// A form that repeats a section contributes one MusicMarkItem per playing, all carrying
// the SAME source position, and until 2026-08-29 the "next rit/accel ends this one" search
// found the second playing and ran the first spanner across every bar in between — six bars
// against one on the reported book (user report 2026-08-29, scratch/ベースタブLy/Untitled-6.lys,
// form A |: B :| A "A2"). Section B is here for the same reason it is there: it is the
// music the first spanner used to run straight through.
// ⚠️ THE TERMINATOR RETIRED THE RULE, NOT THE BOOK (session 289). Pairing in PLAYED order
// cannot make that mistake — playing 1's '@!rit' is reached before playing 2's '@rit' —
// so this book no longer guards a special case; it guards that the ordinary pairing walk
// is not confused by a mark arriving twice. The old spelling had no terminator at all and
// leaned on a one-measure default that has since been retired with it.
// ⚠️ THE TWO DASHED LINES ARE THE ASSERTION, and in THIS book they come out the same
// length to the hundredth (11.29→21.40 and 63.53→73.65): one system, equal bars, so the
// two playings are directly comparable. The reported book's two differ slightly because
// its first bar carries the clef -- there the assertion is one measure each, not one
// number. Before the fix this book drew 11.29→60.58, four bars, against the second's one.
octave absolute
title "rit. in a repeated section"

part m { clef treble }

section A { m { c'4@rit c' c' c' | c'@!rit c' c' c' | } }
section B { m { d'4 d' d' d' | d' d' d' d' | } }

form main { A B A "A2" }

score main { staff m }
